        <!-- ===== NOTES ===== -->
        <mxCell id="notes" value="&lt;div style=&quot;text-align:left;padding:10px&quot;&gt;&lt;b style=&quot;font-size:13px&quot;&gt;Design notes&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size:11px&quot;&gt;1. &lt;b&gt;Appliance mode&lt;/b&gt; on the inspection-VPC TGW attachment pins both directions of a flow to the same AZ â€” required for stateful inspection and coherent bidirectional pcaps.&lt;br/&gt;2. Capture on the &lt;b&gt;decapsulated GENEVE interface (gnv0)&lt;/b&gt;, not the raw ENI. On eth0 every packet looks like GWLBâ†’appliance UDP/6081 and the real flow is buried in the payload.&lt;br/&gt;3. Agents are installed but &lt;b&gt;idle&lt;/b&gt;. Nothing writes to disk until SSM fires; a scheduled TTL stop guarantees captures never run away and fill the volume.&lt;br/&gt;4. Write pcaps to a &lt;b&gt;dedicated EBS volume or tmpfs&lt;/b&gt; â€” never the root volume. Use &lt;code&gt;-s 96&lt;/code&gt; for headers-only unless full payloads are genuinely required.&lt;br/&gt;5. &lt;b&gt;Coverage caveat:&lt;/b&gt; this sees everything crossing the TGW (inter-VPC + egress). Intra-VPC / intra-subnet lateral traffic never reaches the inspection VPC â€” that still needs VPC Traffic Mirroring on specific ENIs, or subnet segmentation forcing lateral flows through the TGW.&lt;br/&gt;6. Prefer the &lt;b&gt;S3 Gateway Endpoint&lt;/b&gt; over the NAT GW for pcap upload â€” NAT data processing (~$0.045/GB) is painful at multi-TB volumes; the gateway endpoint is free.&lt;br/&gt;7. Cross-account bucket access via bucket policy + IAM role; the appliance instance profile needs only &lt;code&gt;s3:PutObject&lt;/code&gt; on its capture-id prefix.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#FFFFFF;strokeColor=#CFD8DC;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="40" y="590" width="1270" height="210" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>

